# Every wrong answer here prints a wrong NUMBER rather than failing, which is
# the whole point of the fixture. Resolving a local means walking the
# environment chain until a name matches; a change that resolves to an index
# instead has to land on the same frame, and an index one out reaches a
# neighbouring binding that is a perfectly good value of the same type. A
# fixture whose locals all held different types, or whose wrong answers
# crashed, would pass for a resolution that was merely wrong.
#
# So the five locals in `ladder` are consecutive integers: off by one in
# either direction prints a neighbour and the golden catches it.
#
# THE GUARD IS THE REASON THIS IS NOT JUST A LIST OF BINDS. `Stmt` has three
# variants and none of them branches, which reads like a body being a flat
# sequence -- and it is not. `Expr::Guard` carries its own statement list and
# evaluates it only when the condition is false, so the binds after a `return
# ... if` line are nested rather than next. `after_guard` puts three of them
# there, under a guard that does not fire.
#
# `shadowed` is the other half, and the checker narrows which half is even
# possible: a local may NOT take the name of a declaration in its own module
# ("`base` is already a declaration; rename the binding"), so the only legal
# shadowing is of a bare-enrolled import. `round` here is the local, and
# `math/round` is what it shadows; reaching past the local finds a function
# value rather than an error, so a wrong answer is again a wrong print.
import "std/math"

fn ladder x
  a = x + 1
  b = x + 2
  c = x + 3
  d = x + 4
  e = x + 5
  "{a} {b} {c} {d} {e}"

fn after_guard n
  return "early" if n < 0
  p = n + 10
  q = n + 20
  r = n + 30
  "{p} {q} {r}"

fn shadowed x
  round = x + 7
  other = x + 8
  "{round} {other}"

pub play = print (ladder 10)
  .> (_ -> print (after_guard 1))
  .> (_ -> print (shadowed 1))
